.TH LS 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
ls \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
.RB [ \-aAcCdfFilmnpqrRstux1 ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ls
writes information about each
.I file
operand, or about the contents of each operand that is a directory,
to standard output. A plain file operand is listed by name; a
directory operand's own contents are listed, sorted, unless
.B \-f
is given. Every plain-file operand is listed first, followed by each
directory operand's listing in turn; a
.RI \(lq name: \(rq
header line precedes a directory's listing whenever more than one
operand was given, or
.B \-R
is recursing. With no operands, the current directory is listed.
.PP
By default, a name beginning with
.RB \(lq . \(rq
is omitted from a directory's listing unless
.B \-a
or
.B \-A
is given. Symbolic links are always reported as themselves (never
followed) when gathering the information a listing needs; this is the
one behavior the standard permits when neither
.B \-H
nor
.B \-L
is in effect, and this build never implements either option to change
it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
List every entry, including those whose name begins with
.RB \(lq . \(rq ,
including
.B .
and
.BR .. .
.TP
.B \-A
Like
.BR \-a ,
but never lists
.B .
or
.BR .. .
.TP
.B \-C
Force multi-column output, sorted down the columns. This is the
default when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
.B \-d
For a directory operand, list the directory entry itself rather than
its contents (as if it were a plain file).
.TP
.B \-f
List a directory's entries in the order stored, unsupported by any
sorting: turns
.B \-a
on and
.BR \-l ", " \-t ", " \-S ,
and
.B \-r
off, regardless of the order these options were given on the command
line.
.TP
.B \-F
Append a marker to certain names: a
.RB \(lq / \(rq
after a directory, a
.RB \(lq * \(rq
after an executable regular file, and, as extensions beyond the
standard's own \(lq/\(rq and \(lq*\(rq markers,
.RB \(lq @ \(rq
after a symbolic link,
.RB \(lq | \(rq
after a FIFO, and
.RB \(lq = \(rq
after a socket.
.TP
.B \-i
Print each file's inode number before its name.
.TP
.B \-l
Long format: one line per entry, giving the mode, link count,
owner, group, size, and modification time, followed by the name. Turns
off
.BR \-n .
.TP
.B \-m
Fill the width of the output with a comma-separated list of names.
Turns off
.BR \-C " and " \-1 .
.TP
.B \-n
Like
.BR \-l ,
but shows the owner and group as raw numeric IDs instead of resolved
names. Turns off
.BR \-l .
.TP
.B \-p
Append a
.RB \(lq / \(rq
after each directory's name; unlike
.BR \-F ,
marks nothing else.
.TP
.B \-q
Replace any non-printable byte in a displayed name with
.RB \(lq ? \(rq .
Applied to every name printed, not only when standard output is a
terminal.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse whatever sort order is otherwise in effect (including the
default name order).
.TP
.B \-R
Recursively list each subdirectory encountered. A directory's own
entries are always listed together, in full, before any of its
subdirectories are descended into and listed in turn, each with its
own
.RI \(lq name: \(rq
header.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the size, in blocks, of each file before its name. Block size
is 512 bytes, or 1024 with
.BR \-k .
.TP
.B \-S
Sort by size, largest first; ties broken by name. Turns off
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-t
Sort by modification time (or, combined with
.BR \-c " or " \-u ,
by the corresponding time field), most recent first; ties broken by
name. Turns off
.BR \-S .
.TP
.B \-u
Use the file's last-access time, instead of its modification time,
both for sorting under
.B \-t
and for display under
.BR \-l .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-c ;
whichever of the two is given later on the command line takes effect.
.TP
.B \-c
Use the file's last-status-change time, instead of its modification
time, both for sorting under
.B \-t
and for display under
.BR \-l .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-u ,
resolved the same way.
.TP
.B \-x
Force multi-column output, sorted across the rows instead of down the
columns. Turns off
.BR \-C ", " \-m ", and " \-1 .
.TP
.B \-1
Force one name per line, with no columns. Turns off
.BR \-C " and " \-m .
.TP
.B \-k
Used with
.BR \-s :
report sizes in 1024-byte blocks instead of 512-byte blocks.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-L ", " \-g ", " \-o
Not implemented. Every symbolic link is always listed as itself
(equivalent to the standard's default, neither-given behavior for
.BR \-H / \-L );
.B \-g
and
.B \-o
(owner-omitted and group-omitted long-format variants) have no
substitute \(em use
.B \-l
or
.B \-n
instead. Giving any of these four options is a usage error.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of a file or directory to list.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLUMNS
When set to a positive integer, used as the display width for laying
out multi-column output (\fB\-C\fR/\fB\-x\fR, and the default column
layout when standard output is a terminal) and
for wrapping
.B \-m
output. Ignored if unset, empty, or not a positive integer, in which
case a width of 80 is assumed. No terminal width is ever queried
directly.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
Owner and group names are resolved only for this process's own user
and group; a file owned by any other numeric ID is shown by that
numeric ID instead of a name, under both
.B \-l
and
.BR \-t .
.PP
Under
.BR \-l / \-n ,
a modification time (or, with
.BR \-c / \-u ,
the corresponding time) within roughly the last six months is shown
as month, day, hour, and minute; an older time, or one in the future,
is shown as month, day, and year instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was listed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An operand could not be accessed, or a directory could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: an invalid or unimplemented option was given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pax (1),
.BR file (1)
